Session 9 
In this session, I realised that evaluating an AI product is not just about checking whether the model is accurate. We need to understand whether 
it creates real business value, protects customer trust, and makes the overall product better.

One point I found interesting was the difference between traditional AI and generative AI. Traditional AI can be more predictable under 
controlled conditions, whereas generative AI may produce different outputs even for similar inputs. This made me realise why testing 
AI products cannot stop after development.

I found the discussion on changing KPIs very relevant. As a product evolves, the metrics used to evaluate it should evolve as well. Revenue growth alone, 
for example, may hide a decline in new customer acquisition or another emerging problem.
This is something I can relate to in automation and AI delivery. It is not enough to report the number of automations 
deployed or the hours saved. We also need to understand whether the process has become more reliable, whether users trust the solution, 
and whether the expected business benefits are actually being realised.

I realised that dashboards and KPIs influence how teams behave. If we reward the wrong measure, people may optimise for the number 
rather than the actual objective.
Going forward, I want to apply this thinking when evaluating automation and AI opportunities: define the expected business outcome, identify the risks 
of incorrect decisions, select a small set of meaningful metrics, and continue monitoring performance after deployment.

My biggest takeaway is that an AI product should not be judged only by how accurately its model performs, but by the business value it creates, 
the risks it manages, and the trust it earns from its users.
